 re-militarize  more seriously  in 2020 japan spent more than 45 billion  euros on its military  that's met with resistance from peace  activists  the trauma of the second world war still  weighs heavily on many  no more war ever again  all my life i fought for that message  but here as elsewhere in the world  russia's war is accelerating re-armament  dw chief political editor michaela kufna  is traveling with chancellor schultz in  japan and she first explained what japan  is doing to aid ukraine  well fumio kishida was adamant that he  perceived japan's duty to help ukraine  to help the ukrainian people they are  sending  goods they're not sending weapons  because they have constraints within  their own constitution and a german  chancellor coming here first is a clear  political symbol that he does not want  to go to china first  he's not going to the most important  economic partner he's going to the  partner of the g7 of those western  democratic values and that's what both  nations have also vowed to stand in for  um further afield also here in the  region they're also doing joint military  exercises so this is all very new ground  both for germany and for japan and they  are seeing what happens in ukraine as a  threat to democracy and freedom  rather than a regional conflict  we'll get back to you in a second  mikhaila but first let's have a listen  to part of what uh olaf shots said at  that press conference that you were at  my trip is a clear political signal that  germany and the european union will  continue and intensify their engagement  in the indo-pacific region  in view of the russian war of aggression  our common focus is currently on ukraine  germany and japan stand side by side in  defending the rules-based international  order in upholding the fundamental  principles of the un charter and in our  commitment to universal human rights  a very clear statement there from shots  mikayla why is it so remarkable that  chancellor schultz would make his 